Audio/pacer frame = real NTSC frame (896040 master, 59.9227 Hz), not 59.94 Hz.

runner/sim_step.c drains the audio chips by GENESIS_SIM_WALL_FRAME_MASTER_CYCLES
= 895780 master cycles per emulated frame, and main.c paces at 60000/1001 =
59.94 fps. Hardware NTSC frame is 262 lines x 3420 master = 896040 cycles
(59.9227 Hz). The Z80 (music tick) and the 68K run the full 262 x 228 / 488
lines, so each frame's audio comes out 260 master cycles short: 3732 PSG
samples per frame instead of ~3733.5.

Measured on Golden Axe (2026-11-12, build with the shift-count fix):
  - recomp --wav: 29,109,600 stereo samples in 7800 frames = exactly 3732.0
    per frame (the fractional leftover is also dropped each frame,
    psg_reset_leftover), against 3733.5 on hardware: -0.040%.
  - That is about -2.9 of the -16.2 frames the audio drifts vs BlastEm over
    116 s (tools/probe/audio_lag.py). The rest is not this patch.
Not covered: the 68K cycle budget per frame stays 262 x 488 = 127,856
(hardware 128,005.7). Separate question, opposite sign.
Risk: the host pacer now runs at 59.9227 fps (was 59.94); both values move
together so the audio buffer stays balanced. --target-fps still overrides.
Test: patches/test_segagenesisrecomp-ntsc-frame-audio.py (static check, plus
--exe check of samples per frame; fails on the current build).
Base: segagenesisrecomp f92e278.
Apply: git -C segagenesisrecomp apply ../patches/segagenesisrecomp-ntsc-frame-audio.patch
